[D-BUS Service]
Name=org.hadal.Broker1
Exec=/usr/libexec/hadal-brokerd
User=root
SystemdService=hadal-brokerd.service

# D-Bus *activation*, which is a different file from the bus policy next to it
# and is the one that was missing.
#
#   system.d/org.hadal.Broker1.conf       who may talk to the name
#   system-services/org.hadal.Broker1.service   how the name comes to exist
#
# With only the first, the bus knows the broker is allowed to own a name that
# nothing provides. Calling it fails with
#
#   org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.ServiceUnknown:
#   The name org.hadal.Broker1 was not provided by any .service files
#
# which reads like a missing package rather than a missing file inside one.
# Found 2026-08-24, on the first `hadal explain` ever run — the CLI worked, the
# spool record was found and read, and the call to the broker failed at the
# last step.
#
# hadal-brokerd.service is already Type=dbus with BusName=org.hadal.Broker1, so
# it was written expecting to be started this way. SystemdService= hands the
# activation to systemd rather than letting dbus-daemon fork the binary itself,
# which is what keeps the unit's sandboxing — ProtectSystem, the capability
# bounding set, the namespace it shares with hadald — applied. A bus-daemon
# fork would run the broker with none of it.
#
# The consequence worth knowing: `systemctl enable hadal-brokerd.service` is
# now optional rather than required. The broker starts when something asks for
# it and not before, which is the right shape for a component that spends most
# of its life idle.
